There's also LogFlash, which is a fairly good tool if you want to  
learn every gismu. It's rather dated but as far as I know still  
matches the current definition of the language.

http://www.lojban.org/files/roadmap.html#software

In general, most learners of Lojban do the Lojban for Beginners course
http://www.tlg.uci.edu/~opoudjis/lojbanbrochure/lessons/book1.html

followed by dipping into or reading The Complete Lojban Language
http://www.lojban.org/tiki/tiki-index.php?page=The+Lojban+Reference 
+Grammar&bl

Neither of these are interactive, but they are more or less the  
standard way to learn the language. A project called ICSL (Intensive  
Course in Spoken Lojban) was working on making a more interactive  
course based on conversations in comic strip format, but it's been  
stalled since December or so.

